        "text": "Patent: US11632938B2\nTitle: Markers for determining the biological age of a dog\nAssignee: Mars, Incorporated\nFiling Date: 2022-06-21\nGrant Date: 2023-04-25\nAbstract: Discloses specific blood and urine biomarkers and methods to compute a biological age or ageing pace for dogs by comparing measured values to reference cohorts.\nOverlaps with paper claims: C2\nTechnical overlap: DAP commits to generating multi-omic datasets and developing predictive biomarkers of canine ageing. The patent claims concretes panels and algorithms that transform clinical biomarkers into an age estimate for dogs, directly aligning with DAP's biomarker development and systems biology modelling objectives. It validates the feasibility of constructing quantitative ageing indices from high-throughput dog data, which DAP aims to produce and benchmark.\n\nPatent: WO2020150705A1\nTitle: DNA methylation measurement for mammals based on conserved loci\nAssignee: Illumina, Inc.; University of California, Berkeley; University of California, San Diego\nFiling Date: 2020-01-20\nGrant Date: Not provided\nAbstract: Describes cross-species DNA methylation arrays and probe design methods targeting conserved CpG loci to enable scalable epigenetic profiling and downstream biomarker construction across mammals.\nOverlaps with paper claims: C2\nTechnical overlap: DAP plans longitudinal epigenome profiling in dogs and to build predictive ageing biomarkers. The patent claims conserved-locus array designs and analysis workflows that make canine methylome measurement practical at scale, which matches DAP's planned methylation assays and modelling. It provides independent IP evidence that cross-species methylation platforms can support age-related biomarker discovery in dogs.\n\nPatent: US11894143B2\nTitle: System and methods for integrating animal health records\nAssignee: Whiskers Worldwide LLC\nFiling Date: 2020-03-06\nGrant Date: 2024-02-06\nAbstract: Claims an integrated pet health platform that ingests veterinary EHR, owner-reported data, device streams and other sources to maintain longitudinal records and generate analytics and recommendations.\nOverlaps with paper claims: C1\nTechnical overlap: DAP's platform aggregates owner surveys, veterinary records, biospecimens and environmental data under structured consent with ongoing updates. The patent claims the same core architecture for multi-source ingestion and longitudinal analytics for companion animals, validating DAP's observational data platform at a systems level. It substantiates the feasibility of nationwide data capture and harmonisation central to DAP's design.\n\nPatent: EP4244859A1\nTitle: Method for performing imputation and/or enrichment of genetic data in an optimised manner\nAssignee: Allelica Srl\nFiling Date: 2021-11-10\nGrant Date: Not provided\nAbstract: Discloses computational workflows for imputation and enrichment of low-coverage sequencing data with adaptive partitioning and quality optimisation.\nOverlaps with paper claims: C2\nTechnical overlap: DAP states it will perform low-pass whole-genome sequencing with imputation for at least 10,000 dogs. The application claims concrete imputation strategies and QC frameworks designed to scale low-coverage data to high-quality variant calls, mapping closely to DAP's planned genetic data processing. This independently developed approach supports the technical viability of DAP's low-pass-plus-imputation strategy.\n\nPatent: US20220401421A1\nTitle: Veterinary formulations comprising rapamycin and methods of using the same for treating animal disease\nAssignee: Triviumvet DAC\nFiling Date: 2020-12-18\nGrant Date: Not provided\nAbstract: Claims rapamycin and rapalog formulations and veterinary dosing regimens for companion animals to treat cardiac dysfunction, cancer, inflammatory disease and ageing effects.\nOverlaps with paper claims: C3\nTechnical overlap: DAP's TRIAD is a randomised, double-masked, placebo-controlled trial of weekly low-dose rapamycin in middle-aged large-breed dogs with endpoints in lifespan, cardiac and cognitive function. The patent claims veterinary-specific rapamycin compositions and methods, including indications tied to age-related cardiac health, which mirror TRIAD's intervention and measurement focus. This provides independent evidence that clinical translation of rapamycin for canine healthspan is being actively pursued and protected.",
