        "text": "=== OVERALL ASSESSMENT ===\nThe paper's central narrative aligns directionally with broader literature: most cancer-relevant lncRNA signals appear cancer-type specific with a small shared core, and a subset of lncRNAs can encode micropeptides detectable in tumours. The most specific elements-cross-cancer, metastasis-stage-coherent behaviour of named Tr-lncRNAs (especially XIST and USP9Y) and quantitative feature-enrichment magnitudes-remain preliminary and rely largely on the authors' analysis. Independent replication, biotype disambiguation, and fully re-runnable public pipelines would substantially strengthen verification.\n\n=== VERIFIED CLAIMS ===\n\nClaim: C1\nOriginal: Most Tr-lncRNAs that mark cancer progression are cancer-type specific, with minimal sharing across cancers.\nStatus: Partially Verified\nConfidence: Moderate\n\nSupporting Evidence:\n  - [Similar Paper] Pan-cancer transcriptomic analysis associates long non-coding RNAs with key mutational driver events\n    Reports that many lncRNA signals are tissue-specific with only a subset reproducible across cancers, supporting cancer-type specificity.\n  - [Similar Paper] LncMAP: Pan-cancer atlas of long noncoding RNA-mediated transcriptional network perturbations\n    Shows lncRNA modulators cluster by tissue of origin with only a fraction acting pan-cancer, indicating limited cross-cancer sharing.\n  - [Similar Paper] Comprehensive characterization of lncRNA-mRNA related ceRNA network across 12 major cancers\n    Quantifies cross-cancer sharing and identifies a small conserved subnetwork, consistent with a small shared core.\n  - [Patent] US20230348994A1: Identification of Immune-related lncRNA Signatures with Prognostic Connotation in Multi-Cancer Setting\n    Describes per-cancer lncRNA panels with selective cross-cancer portability, aligning with predominantly cancer-type specific signals.\n\nContradicting Evidence:\n  - [Similar Paper] LncMAP: Pan-cancer atlas of long noncoding RNA-mediated transcriptional network perturbations\n    Also reports a notable set of pan-cancer modulators, suggesting more sharing than minimal depending on definitions.\n\nSummary: Multiple independent pan-cancer studies show lncRNA signals are largely cancer-type specific with only a modest shared core, consistent with the claim. Some analyses also highlight a meaningful pan-cancer subset, so the degree of sharing is method-dependent and not absolute.\n\nClaim: C2\nOriginal: A small pan-cancer subset of Tr-lncRNAs, notably XIST and USP9Y, shows coherent trends across metastatic transitions, with XIST exhibiting moderate cross-cancer correlation between primary growth and metastasis transitions.\nStatus: Partially Verified\nConfidence: Low\n\nSupporting Evidence:\n  - [Similar Paper] Transitional lncRNA Signatures Reveal Distinct Stages of Cancer Progression and Metastasis\n    Authors' own cross-cancer analysis identifies a small shared set and highlights XIST and USP9Y among pan-cancer hits for stage transitions.\n  - [Patent] CN111893190A: Application of lncRNA XIST as a diagnostic marker for gastric cancer\n    Independent clinical biomarker use of XIST supports broad relevance consistent with inclusion in a shared subset.\n\nSummary: Independent literature supports the existence of a small pan-cancer lncRNA subset, but consistent metastasis-stage coherence for XIST across cancers is not firmly established. USP9Y raises gene biotype concerns and cross-cancer, metastasis-stage trends lack independent replication, so confidence is low.\n\nClaim: C3\nOriginal: Tr-lncRNAs display distinct molecular features versus the background lncRNA set, including longer transcripts, more exons and transcripts, and about twofold higher small-protein coding capacity, with many feature differences reaching FDR significance.\nStatus: Partially Verified\nConfidence: Low\n\nSupporting Evidence:\n  - [Similar Paper] Pan-cancer proteogenomic analysis reveals long and circular noncoding RNAs encoding peptides\n    Provides proteomic evidence that many lncRNAs encode detectable peptides in tumours, supporting elevated small-protein capacity among cancer-relevant lncRNAs.\n  - [Similar Paper] CrypticProteinDB: an integrated database of proteome and immunopeptidome derived non-canonical cancer proteins\n    Catalogues peptide support for thousands of non-canonical ORFs in noncoding RNAs across cancers, reinforcing widespread lncRNA translation.\n  - [Patent] US20240060071A1: Treatment of cancer associated with dysregulated novel open reading frame products\n    Therapeutic targeting of lncRNA-derived micropeptides corroborates functional small-protein coding capacity relevant to cancer.\n\nSummary: There is strong evidence that some lncRNAs encode micropeptides in cancer, supporting the direction of the claim. However, the specific feature-enrichment pattern for transitional lncRNAs versus background and the approximate twofold magnitude have not been independently replicated.\n",
